<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch7-page1-symmetric-games" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Symmetric Games</title>

  <p>
    This chapter is short: we only need one idea from it, symmetric games,
    because it sets up everything we'll do with evolutionary stability in
    Chapter 8.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-7-1">
    <title>Section 7.1, Interchangeable Players</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 7.1.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      Focus on the idea, not the notation. You don't need to master the
      notation with permutations. The main point is this: a two-player game
      is <term>symmetric</term> if both players have the same strategies,
      and swapping the players just swaps their payoffs. In symbols,
      <me>\pi_1(s, t) = \pi_2(t, s)</me>
      for all strategies <m>s</m> and <m>t</m>. In a payoff table, this
      means the entry in row <m>s</m>, column <m>t</m> is the entry in row
      <m>t</m>, column <m>s</m> with the two payoffs switched.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Here is the main result of the section, restated.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-7-1-restated">
    <title>Theorem 7.1</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Every symmetric game with finitely many pure strategies has a
        <term>symmetric Nash equilibrium</term>, one in which every player
        uses the same (possibly mixed) strategy. For a two-player game, this
        is a Nash equilibrium of the form <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    This is essential for Chapter 8. There, we'll think of a game as being
    played over and over by members of a large population, and a
    population state <m>\sigma</m> describes how often each strategy is
    used. Evolutionary stability is about Nash equilibria of the form
    <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m>, and Theorem 7.1 guarantees that every
    symmetric game has at least one.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Canonical games                                              -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    Some games keep coming back, in this course and in game theory in
    general. Here is a reminder of four canonical symmetric games we've
    already seen. In each table, the pure strategy Nash equilibria are
    boxed.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="ch7-canonical-pd">
    <title>Prisoner's Dilemma</title>
    <p>
      Each player can cooperate (<m>c</m>) or defect (<m>d</m>).
    </p>
  <table xml:id="table-ch7-pd">
    <title>Prisoner's Dilemma</title>
    <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Player 2: <m>c</m></cell>
        <cell>Player 2: <m>d</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>Player 1: <m>c</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(-1, -1)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(-10, 0)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Player 1: <m>d</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(0, -10)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\boxed{(-6, -6)}</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
    <p>
      Defecting is a strictly dominant strategy, so <m>(d, d)</m> is the
      only Nash equilibrium, even though both players would be better off
      if they both cooperated. It is already symmetric.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="ch7-canonical-stag">
    <title>Stag Hunt</title>
    <p>
      Each hunter can hunt a stag together with the other hunter, or hunt a
      hare alone.
    </p>
  <table xml:id="table-ch7-stag">
    <title>Stag Hunt</title>
    <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Hunter 2: stag</cell>
        <cell>Hunter 2: hare</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>Hunter 1: stag</cell>
        <cell><m>\boxed{(2, 2)}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(0, 1)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Hunter 1: hare</cell>
        <cell><m>(1, 0)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\boxed{(1, 1)}</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
    <p>
      Both Nash equilibria are symmetric. The hunters agree that
      (stag, stag) is better, but hunting stag is risky if you aren't sure
      the other hunter will join you.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="ch7-canonical-chicken">
    <title>Chicken</title>
    <p>
      Each teenager can go straight or swerve.
    </p>
  <table xml:id="table-ch7-chicken">
    <title>Chicken</title>
    <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Teenager 2: straight</cell>
        <cell>Teenager 2: swerve</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>Teenager 1: straight</cell>
        <cell><m>(-2, -2)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\boxed{(1, -1)}</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Teenager 1: swerve</cell>
        <cell><m>\boxed{(-1, 1)}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
    <p>
      Each teenager wants to be the one who goes straight while the other
      swerves, so the two pure Nash equilibria are <em>not</em> symmetric.
      The symmetric Nash equilibrium promised by Theorem 7.1 is mixed: each
      teenager goes straight with probability <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="ch7-canonical-water">
    <title>Water Pollution</title>
    <p>
      Three firms each either purify their waste (<m>g</m>) or pollute
      (<m>b</m>). Purifying costs 1, and if two or more firms pollute, the
      water is polluted and each firm pays 3. Since there are three
      players, we use two tables, one for each choice of Firm 3, with
      payoffs listed as (Firm 1, Firm 2, Firm 3).
    </p>
  <table xml:id="table-ch7-water-g">
    <title>Water Pollution, Firm 3 purifies</title>
    <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Firm 2: <m>g</m></cell>
        <cell>Firm 2: <m>b</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>Firm 1: <m>g</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(-1, -1, -1)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\boxed{(-1, 0, -1)}</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Firm 1: <m>b</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\boxed{(0, -1, -1)}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(-3, -3, -4)</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
  <table xml:id="table-ch7-water-b">
    <title>Water Pollution, Firm 3 pollutes</title>
    <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Firm 2: <m>g</m></cell>
        <cell>Firm 2: <m>b</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>Firm 1: <m>g</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\boxed{(-1, -1, 0)}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(-4, -3, -3)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Firm 1: <m>b</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(-3, -4, -3)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\boxed{(-3, -3, -3)}</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
    <p>
      In the pure Nash equilibria where two firms purify and one pollutes,
      the polluter gets clean water for free. These aren't symmetric. The
      symmetric one is the worst outcome: all three firms pollute, and
      nobody wants to be the only one paying to purify water that stays
      polluted anyway.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="check-chicken-symmetric">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Chicken is a symmetric game, but neither of its pure strategy Nash
        equilibria is symmetric. Does this contradict Theorem 7.1?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Yes, because Theorem 7.1 says there is a symmetric Nash equilibrium.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Theorem 7.1 allows the symmetric Nash equilibrium to
            be mixed.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>No, because the symmetric Nash equilibrium can be mixed.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. In Chicken, the symmetric Nash equilibrium is
            <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> where each teenager goes straight with
            probability <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-7-1" iframe="question-form.html?section=7.1" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
